Hi both - responses are below.

{quote}_We don't have a company deletion process because companies_ *_don't_* _full under GDPR. So from our standpoint we should be covered with the "deactivation request by fleet owner" suspension, which I see is already applied. From fleet owner perspective, this is the end of it as it is an irreversible process._{quote}

Correct - We are only dealing with deletion of the fleet owner profile (i.e., profile of an individual, rather than a company). This is the extent of our GDPR obligations. If the fleet owner account is deactivated, then this is sufficient - {color:#4c9aff}*on what date was this actioned*{color}?

{quote}_the information we are required to retain is extensive…It seems to cover all of the information we currently hold…Am I missing something about this, or are we going to retain everything we already have on the fleet manager?_{quote}

This is a fair point to raise, and is something we are aware of. The GDPR requires us to delete all the user’s personal data unless we have a good reason to retain it (in which case, we need to flag this to the user). DAC7 and the DSA effectively require us to retain all of the user’s data except for very minor datapoints like date of birth. To mitigate the risk that the user will not be happy that we are retaining so much of their data (which we are entitled to do), we therefore archive the account so at least their account is no longer active / externally visible / open to being used by anyone else. In short - we are retaining most of their data, yes, but archiving the account helps to mitigate any negative reaction by the user.

Does that all make sense? Happy to have a call if anything is unclear 🙂

