[~accountid:60b0a48fd435380069b90208], [~accountid:63da287e790148a18093a774], a few thoughts on this topic from my side:

a) it is highly unlikely that the State Tax Inspectorate (VMI) will confirm whether it is acceptable for us to stop checking Individual Activity Certificates, as this does not fall within the scope of their responsibilities. Such decisions are typically based on the internal processes of each company, including its established due diligence procedures. While VMI is not obligated to provide guidance on this matter, we may continue to request their input, as having a written opinion from them would certainly be beneficial. A confirmation from VMI could help mitigate any associated risks and provide us with added assurance;
b) checking contractors' Individual Activity Certificates ensures that they are legally registered, compliant with local regulations, and authorized to carry out their business activities. Verifying these documents not only confirms their legal standing but also fosters transparency and helps ensure that the company’s operations are fully compliant with applicable laws and industry standards;
c) when entering into contracts with legal entities, it is always advisable to review their statutory documents to ensure that they are legally authorized to enter into and fulfill the terms of contracts. This includes verifying their registration, legal status, and any relevant licenses or permits. The same principle and approach should apply when engaging with independent contractors (which are food couriers). It is important to verify that they hold the necessary certifications, registrations, or licenses required to perform their activity, in order to enhance compliance. 

The discussed due diligence when checking Individual Activity Certificates helps mitigate potential legal / tax related risks as well as contributes to the transparency of Bolt’s business, which is essential given the increasing attention to the company’s operations by public authorities. Even though this process requires lots of resources and time, I would only support the position to stop reviewing the Individual Activity Certificates subject to such approach is confirmed by VMI in a written manner, which, as mentioned previously, is highly unlikely to happen.

