Under these conditions Separate Data Processing Agreement would most likely have to be signed, providing them anonymised data would require huge effort from infosec team (as we would have to do the data-exploration portion of the research by ourselves).

{quote}3.a) Could you please clarify the agreed approach and what specific anonymisation techniques will be used?{quote}

This heavily depends on what data they would like to use for publication, it could be sample set of manually censored phishing messages. It can be impersonal statistics (e.g percentage of orders with phishing, success rate of phishing messages + cohorting based on variables such as country, age of user, etc..).

{quote}3.b) Who on the research team will have access to raw data, and what safeguards are in place to prevent unauthorised access? Will access be logged and monitored?{quote}

Raw data would be handled by the primary researcher + (potentially) 1-2 research assistants. From infosec perspective we would like them to be onboarded as contractors with Bolt accounts and equipment for the duration of data access, they would not be allowed to export raw data.

{quote}3.c) How long will the anonymised data be retained, and what are the proposed deletion procedures?{quote}

From purely research perspective, sufficiently anonymised data (e.g generic statistcs) could be made publicly available. However we haven’t discussed it in detail yet, guidance from Privacy is very-much welcome at this point.

I will discuss your clarifications with the researcher and will come back to you once I have gotten their answers.

